1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,200
Okay, so today I want to talk about the single most common reason home sourdough fails.

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:10,400
And it is not your starter. It is almost never your starter.

3
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:16,800
The biggest mistake people make with sourdough is that they rush the bulk ferment.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,600
They read four hours in a recipe, they set a timer for four hours, and they move on.

5
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:28,200
But four hours is not an instruction. It is one baker's kitchen on one particular day.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,400
Here's the thing nobody tells you: dough does not measure time. Dough measures temperature.

7
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,800
A dough at twenty-two degrees and a dough at twenty-six degrees are running two different clocks.

8
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:46,200
Four degrees will roughly halve your bulk ferment. That is the whole trick.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:52,400
So stop watching the clock and start watching the dough. Which means you need a way to see it.

10
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:58,600
Take a small straight-sided jar. Pull off a walnut of dough after mixing and drop it in.

11
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:04,800
Press it flat, mark the top with a rubber band, and set it next to your main bowl.

12
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:10,200
That jar is now a live gauge. It sees exactly the temperature your dough sees.

13
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,600
When the sample has risen by seventy-five percent, your bulk ferment is done. Not before.

14
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:22,800
Not at four hours. Not when the recipe says. When the jar says. Every single time.

15
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:28,400
Now, the second mistake, and this one is sneakier, is what people do next.

16
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:34,600
They dump the dough out, they shape it hard, and they knock every bit of gas out of it.

17
00:01:35,200 --> 00:01:40,800
All that fermentation you just waited for, gone, in about fifteen seconds of aggressive handling.

18
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:46,200
Shaping is not kneading. Shaping is building tension on the surface and nothing else.

19
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:52,400
You want a tight skin and a soft interior. Firm hands on the outside, gentle everywhere else.

20
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:58,800
If your loaf comes out dense with a tight crumb, this is usually where it died.

21
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:04,600
Third: everybody underproofs the final rise because they are scared of overproofing.

22
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:10,400
And I get it, an overproofed loaf is a genuinely sad object. It spreads like a pancake.

23
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:16,600
But underproofed bread is the far more common failure, and it looks like success going in.

24
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:22,800
The poke test is how you tell. Flour a finger, press the dough about a centimetre deep.

25
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:28,600
If it springs straight back, it needs more time. If it stays dented forever, you are late.

26
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:34,800
What you want is slow. It should come back most of the way and leave a shallow mark.

27
00:02:35,400 --> 00:02:40,200
That slow, lazy spring is ready. Turn it out and bake it right then.

28
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:46,600
Which brings me to the oven, and the one piece of equipment that actually matters.

29
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:52,800
It is not a fancy oven. It is a lid. Anything that traps steam around the loaf.

30
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:58,600
A dutch oven, a roasting tin turned upside down, a stainless bowl over a stone. All fine.

31
00:02:59,200 --> 00:03:04,800
Steam keeps the crust soft for the first fifteen minutes so the loaf can actually expand.

32
00:03:05,400 --> 00:03:10,600
Take the lid off too early and the crust sets, and whatever height you had is the height you get.

33
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:16,800
Twenty minutes covered, twenty uncovered, and do not open the door to look. Trust it.

34
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:22,600
So: watch the jar, not the clock. Shape gently. Proof until the dough is lazy.

35
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:28,800
Do those three things and your bread gets better this week, with the starter you already have.

36
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:34,000
That is the whole video. Go and mark a jar. I will see you next time.
